# =============================================================================
# GTS Algo Research — example environment. Copy to ".env" and fill in the placeholders.
#
# PLACEHOLDERS ONLY: every secret below is empty or an obviously-fake example.
# Never commit a real .env. Only this .env.example is tracked in git.
#
# SAFE BY DEFAULT: a verbatim copy of this file starts GTS Algo Research in
# paper_latency with live trading disabled at BOTH deployment gates AND both
# per-broker gates. Turning on real orders requires deliberately flipping
# several independent switches AND arming the runtime — see docs/CONFIGURATION.md
# and src/box/LIVE_EXECUTION.md.
#
# Defaults shown in comments are the values the code falls back to when the
# variable is unset; they were read from the source, not guessed.
# =============================================================================
#
# THE FOUR AUTHORITIES. This file holds TWO of them; know which you are editing.
#
# 213 variables are declared below and every one is genuinely read by src/**. They
# are not, however, the same KIND of thing, and conflating them is what makes routine
# operation error-prone:
#
#   1. SECRETS              credentials. Tagged [SECRET] below and belonging in the
#                           protected secrets file, never here. Never exposed through
#                           any API, status payload, log line or diagnostic.
#
#   2. DEPLOYMENT CAPABILITY what this deployment is ALLOWED to do at all:
#                           BOX_EXECUTION_MODE, BOX_LIVE_TRADING_ENABLED,
#                           ZERODHA_LIVE_TRADING_ENABLED, DHAN_LIVE_TRADING_ENABLED,
#                           BOX_SHADOW_MODE_ENABLED, BOX_EXECUTION_COORDINATOR_ENABLED.
#                           ENV ONLY, permanently. No runtime control can widen these,
#                           and the operator UI can display them but never write them.
#                           A runtime control may only REMOVE permission underneath
#                           them.
#
#   3. INFRASTRUCTURE        PORT, NODE_ENV, APP_TIMEZONE, FRONTEND_URL, the database
#                           DSNs and pool settings, Mongo export, trusted proxies,
#                           deployment identity, broker API/auth roots, transport
#                           internals, token-provider plumbing, shutdown. Server-owned.
#                           These do not belong on a trading screen.
#
#   4. OPERATOR POLICY       strategy thresholds, risk ceilings, market-data freshness
#                           and coherence bounds, paper/calibration settings, scanner
#                           limits. These are the ones an operator legitimately changes
#                           DURING a trading day, and needing a file edit plus a restart
#                           to change one is the problem docs/OPERATOR_CONFIG.md solves.
#
# ABOUT (4), AND WHY NOTHING BELOW HAS BEEN MARKED OPTIONAL YET.
#
# A backend-owned, PostgreSQL-persisted runtime configuration subsystem exists
# (src/box/operatorConfig/, migration 013, GET/PATCH /api/box/operator-config in the
# contract). Once its engine wiring lands, roughly fifty of the BOX_* variables below
# become BOOTSTRAP DEFAULTS: still read, still honoured when nothing is persisted, and
# for the safety ceilings still an ABSOLUTE MAXIMUM that a runtime value may tighten
# but never raise.
#
# That wiring is NOT yet landed — see docs/OPERATOR_CONFIG.md section 7. Until it is,
# the environment genuinely IS the only source for every value here, so nothing below
# has been regrouped, removed or marked LEGACY. The file operators copy is the worst
# possible place to be premature about which variables still matter.
#
# WHEN IT LANDS, the precedence will be:
#
#     code default  ->  this file  ->  persisted operator override
#                                      (never widening a ceiling set here)
#
# and `GET /api/box/operator-config` reports, per setting, which layer won. See
# docs/OPERATOR_CONFIG_AUDIT.md for the per-variable classification.
#
# =============================================================================
#
# TWO FILES, NOT ONE. READ THIS BEFORE COPYING.
#
# Configuration is split by SENSITIVITY, because the two halves have different
# lifecycles and very different blast radii:
#
#   /etc/gts/secrets.env   CREDENTIALS ONLY, mode 0600, OUTSIDE this repository.
#                          Broker keys, the token-encryption key, the site
#                          passcode, database DSNs. Written once per rotation and
#                          never printed. Every entry below tagged [SECRET]
#                          belongs here.
#
#   .env  (this file)      OPERATIONAL CONFIGURATION. Risk limits, feed freshness
#                          windows, execution mode, scanner and strike settings.
#                          An operator edits it between sessions; a deployment
#                          must never overwrite it, because losing it means
#                          losing the tuning of a live trading system.
#
# PRECEDENCE, highest first:
#
#   1. the explicit process environment   (what a deploy or your shell exported)
#   2. /etc/gts/secrets.env               (credentials)
#   3. .env                               (this file)
#   4. code defaults
#
# Nothing lower ever overwrites something higher, so a credential injected at
# deploy time cannot be shadowed by a stale copy in a file. A variable that is
# present but BLANK counts as absent, so an injected `FOO=` does not mask a real
# value further down. See src/env/load.ts and docs/SECRETS_AND_CONFIG.md.
#
# LEGEND for the tags used below:
#   [SECRET · required: LIVE]   fatal at startup when live trading is enabled
#   [SECRET · required: only …] needed by one script or one optional feature
#   [SECRET · OPTIONAL]         absence just disables something
#   everything untagged         non-secret operational configuration
#
# Putting a credential in .env still WORKS — the loader reads it — but it is
# reported loudly at every startup, because .env is the file people open, paste
# from and copy to a laptop while debugging.
# =============================================================================

# Path to the protected secrets file. CONFIG, not a secret — a path is not a
# credential.
#
# It CANNOT be set in this file or in the secrets file: the loader resolves it
# from the real process environment before any file is parsed, because a file
# cannot name the file that must be read in order to find it. Set it in
# ecosystem.config.cjs (the PM2 `env` block) if you move the file, so it survives
# `pm2 resurrect` after a reboot. Leave it alone to use the default.
#
# GTS_SECRETS_FILE=/etc/gts/secrets.env


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Application (src/config.ts)
#
# Process-level settings. Configuration is validated at boot and fails closed
# with the full list of problems, so an operator fixing .env at 08:55 IST sees
# every error in one pass.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# TCP port this backend listens on. Default 3001. Must be 1..65535.
PORT=3001

# development | test | production. Default development. `production` only ever
# tightens (e.g. it requires FRONTEND_URL to be https because the session cookie
# is Secure-only).
NODE_ENV=development

# MUST be Asia/Kolkata. Every trading-day, token-scheduling and P&L-day decision
# is an IST decision; any other value is rejected at boot rather than honoured.
APP_TIMEZONE=Asia/Kolkata

# Exact origin the browser frontend runs on. Required. CORS is exact-origin WITH
# credentials — never a wildcard. Use 127.0.0.1 (not localhost) locally so it
# matches cookie and origin checks. Must be https in production.
FRONTEND_URL=http://127.0.0.1:5173

# How long (ms) graceful shutdown may take before it is force-stopped. Default
# 20000. Minimum 1000. PM2 `kill_timeout` must sit comfortably above this so the
# shutdown sequence can finish before SIGKILL (see ecosystem.config.cjs).
SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_MS=20000


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Site access (src/config.ts, src/access/*)
#
# A single site passcode gates the whole UI. IMPORTANT: the passcode ARMS
# NOTHING — it lets an operator into the site; it does NOT enable live trading.
# Live trading needs the deployment gates + per-broker gate + runtime arming.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The site passcode. Required to reach the GTS Box workspace.
#
# BACKEND-ONLY, ALWAYS. This value is read by the backend and NOWHERE ELSE. It is never
# sent to the browser, never returned by any API (a wrong passcode and an unset secret
# produce an identical 401 body, on purpose), never logged, and it has no `VITE_`
# counterpart — the frontend bundle is public, and CI fails the build if the string
# "SITE_ACCESS_SECRET" appears anywhere in it.
#
# FAILS CLOSED. If this is unset, `POST /api/access/verify` cannot succeed for ANY input:
# no session is minted and the workspace is unreachable. That is the intended behaviour —
# an unconfigured deployment is a locked one, not an open one.
#
# Use a long random string, e.g. `openssl rand -base64 48`.
# [SECRET · required: LIVE (fatal). Outside live it only warns — the gate fails closed.]
# → belongs in the PROTECTED SECRETS FILE (/etc/gts/secrets.env, mode 0600), NOT in .env.
SITE_ACCESS_SECRET=<strong-random-secret>

# Session lifetime in hours. Default 24. Must be > 0 and <= 720 (30 days).
SITE_SESSION_TTL_HOURS=24

# Name of the HttpOnly session cookie. Default gts_session. Letters, digits,
# underscore and hyphen only.
#
# SETTING IT EXPLICITLY (as here) IS THE RECOMMENDED PRODUCTION PRACTICE: the value
# is then pinned by your own configuration and a future change to the code's default
# cannot log your operators out. Changing THIS value on an existing deployment does
# log everyone out once — the browser keeps presenting the old cookie name and the
# server no longer looks for it. Session rows are untouched and simply expire.
SESSION_COOKIE_NAME=gts_session

# Origin required on mutating (CSRF-sensitive) requests. Defaults to FRONTEND_URL
# when unset. Must be an absolute http(s) origin.
CSRF_ALLOWED_ORIGIN=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PostgreSQL — the single operational authority (src/pg/pool.ts, src/config.ts)
#
# PostgreSQL is NOT optional. It is the durable authority for order intents,
# reservations, trades and sessions. A local Unix socket or a localhost TCP URL
# both work.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# libpq connection URL. Required. Examples:
#   postgres:///strikedge                         (local Unix socket, peer auth)
#   postgresql://strikedge:PASSWORD@127.0.0.1:5432/strikedge
#
# THE ROLE / DATABASE NAME IS DELIBERATELY UNCHANGED BY THE REBRAND. It names a real
# PostgreSQL role and database that existing deployments already hold every order
# intent, reservation, trade and session in. Renaming a live database is a migration
# with downtime, not a rebrand — and pointing this URL at a new, empty database
# would look like a clean start while the real trading history sat untouched
# elsewhere. Choose whatever names you like for a NEW deployment; do not change
# this on an existing one without a planned migration.
# [SECRET · required: LIVE (fatal). Carries the database password.]
# → belongs in the PROTECTED SECRETS FILE (/etc/gts/secrets.env, mode 0600), NOT in .env.
DATABASE_URL=postgres:///strikedge

# Max pooled connections. Default 10. Must be > 0.
PG_POOL_MAX=10

# Per-statement timeout (ms), applied to every pooled connection. Default 5000.
# A wedged statement in the execution path is worse than a failed one.
PG_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# Lock-acquisition timeout (ms). Default 2000.
PG_LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS=2000

# Apply pending SQL migrations during boot. Default true (documented procedure
# for the single-process PM2 fork; the advisory lock makes a simultaneous second
# starter safe). Set false to require `npm run migrate` as an explicit release
# step — boot then refuses if the schema is behind.
PG_MIGRATE_ON_BOOT=true


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Token encryption (src/brokerState/tokenCrypto.ts)
#
# AES-256-GCM at-rest encryption of broker access tokens.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# MUST decode to EXACTLY 32 bytes. Accepted encodings: 64-char hex, OR base64 /
# base64url that decodes to 32 bytes. Anything else is rejected with a specific
# reason. Generate one with:  openssl rand -hex 32
# Losing/rotating this makes previously-sealed tokens unreadable (they are
# re-acquired next morning) — see docs/RUNBOOK.md and rotate:broker-token-key.
# [SECRET · required: LIVE (fatal). Shape is checked too: must decode to exactly 32 bytes.]
# → belongs in the PROTECTED SECRETS FILE (/etc/gts/secrets.env, mode 0600), NOT in .env.
BROKER_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHERE ACCESS TOKENS COME FROM (src/index.ts, src/brokerAuthRoutes.ts)
#
# in_app   (DEFAULT) The operator signs in to each broker FROM THIS DEPLOYMENT.
#          POST /api/broker/{zerodha|dhan}/login/start returns the broker's own
#          consent URL; the browser authenticates at the broker; the broker
#          redirects back to GET /api/broker/{broker}/callback; this backend
#          exchanges the one-time code for an access token, seals it with
#          BROKER_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY and stores it in PostgreSQL. The external
#          token poller does NOT run in this mode.
#
# provider The historical behaviour: poll the external CalSpread token routes
#          from BROKER_TOKEN_POLL_START. Set this if you still provision tokens
#          from another service and do not want in-app sign-in.
#
# The two modes are mutually exclusive ON PURPOSE. A poller running alongside an
# interactive login can overwrite the session an operator just established, so
# only one mechanism ever owns a token.
#
# BOTH brokers can hold a session AT THE SAME TIME in either mode (one row each
# in broker_sessions). Which one actually TRADES is a separate, blocker-checked
# decision made via POST /api/broker/select — signing in to a broker never makes
# it active, and switching the active broker never touches the other's token.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
BROKER_LOGIN_MODE=in_app


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Zerodha (Kite Connect) app credentials — REQUIRED FOR in_app SIGN-IN
# (src/brokers/zerodha/auth.ts)
#
# Create a Kite Connect app at https://developers.kite.trade/apps and set its
# REDIRECT URL to this backend's callback, exactly:
#
#     https://<your-api-host>/api/broker/zerodha/callback
#
# Kite ALWAYS redirects to the URL registered on the app and ignores any
# redirect we send, so a mismatch here is the single most common cause of a
# login that never completes.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Public. Appears in the consent URL and in the Authorization header.
# [SECRET · required: LIVE when DEFAULT_ACTIVE_BROKER=zerodha and BROKER_LOGIN_MODE=in_app.]
# → belongs in the PROTECTED SECRETS FILE (/etc/gts/secrets.env, mode 0600), NOT in .env.
KITE_API_KEY=

# SECRET. Used only to compute sha256(api_key + request_token + api_secret)
# inside this process. Never sent as a query parameter, never logged, never
# returned by any API. Leave empty for a Dhan-only or paper deployment.
# [SECRET · required: LIVE when DEFAULT_ACTIVE_BROKER=zerodha and BROKER_LOGIN_MODE=in_app.]
# → belongs in the PROTECTED SECRETS FILE (/etc/gts/secrets.env, mode 0600), NOT in .env.
KITE_API_SECRET=

# The redirect URL you registered on the Kite app. INFORMATIONAL for this
# backend (Kite uses its own registered copy) — it is read only so the status
# surface can show operators where the browser will land.
KITE_REDIRECT_URL=

# Optional endpoint overrides. Leave empty unless Zerodha moves a host; the
# defaults are the documented production endpoints. Tests point these at a
# loopback mock, which is why they are configurable at all.
#   KITE_LOGIN_URL default https://kite.zerodha.com/connect/login
#   KITE_API_ROOT  default https://api.kite.trade
KITE_LOGIN_URL=
KITE_API_ROOT=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dhan consent (browser login) endpoints — REQUIRED FOR in_app SIGN-IN
# (src/brokers/dhan/auth.ts)
#
# Register this backend's callback as the app's REDIRECT URL, exactly:
#
#     https://<your-api-host>/api/broker/dhan/callback
#
# WHY THESE PATHS ARE CONFIGURABLE: Dhan has shipped the consent flow under two
# naming schemes, and which one your credentials speak depends on how the app
# was registered. The defaults below are the "app" variant. If generate-consent
# 404s or returns no consent id, switch to the "partner" variant by setting:
#
#     DHAN_CONSENT_GENERATE_PATH=/partner/generate-consent
#     DHAN_CONSENT_CONSUME_PATH=/partner/consume-consent
#     DHAN_CONSENT_LOGIN_URL=https://auth.dhan.co/consent-login
#     DHAN_CONSENT_ID_PARAM=consentId
#     DHAN_AUTH_ID_HEADER=partner_id
#     DHAN_AUTH_SECRET_HEADER=partner_secret
#
# See docs/BROKER_TOKENS.md for the full flow.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The redirect URL you registered on the Dhan app. Informational here, for the
# same reason as KITE_REDIRECT_URL.
DHAN_REDIRECT_URL=

# Optional order-postback URL registered on the Dhan app. Not used by the login.
DHAN_POSTBACK_URL=

# Auth host. Default https://auth.dhan.co. Must be https outside tests.
DHAN_AUTH_ROOT=

# Consent endpoint paths and the browser login URL. Defaults:
#   DHAN_CONSENT_GENERATE_PATH  /app/generate-consent
#   DHAN_CONSENT_CONSUME_PATH   /app/consumeApp-consent
#   DHAN_CONSENT_LOGIN_URL      {DHAN_AUTH_ROOT}/login/consentApp-login
#   DHAN_CONSENT_ID_PARAM       consentAppId
DHAN_CONSENT_GENERATE_PATH=
DHAN_CONSENT_CONSUME_PATH=
DHAN_CONSENT_LOGIN_URL=
DHAN_CONSENT_ID_PARAM=

# Header names carrying the app id / app SECRET on the two consent calls.
# Defaults app_id / app_secret.
DHAN_AUTH_ID_HEADER=
DHAN_AUTH_SECRET_HEADER=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TOKEN EXPOSURE — let OTHER SERVERS fetch the token this deployment minted
# (src/tokenExposureRoutes.ts)
#
#     GET /api/tokens/zerodha     header: x-token-access-key: <TOKEN_EXPOSURE_KEY>
#     GET /api/tokens/dhan        header: x-token-access-key: <TOKEN_EXPOSURE_KEY>
#
# Response: { broker, access_token, identity, login_date, expires_at, active,
#             fetched_at }.  `identity` is what the token must be PAIRED with:
# the Kite api_key for Zerodha, the dhan client id for Dhan.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE ENABLING. These are the ONLY two routes in this backend that
# return a plaintext access token; everywhere else that is a hard invariant.
# They exist so sibling services do not each run their own broker login.
#
#   * OFF BY DEFAULT. Unset ⇒ every request answers 503. You must opt in.
#   * A key shorter than 32 characters is REFUSED (503), not honoured — a
#     guessable key here is equivalent to publishing the token.
#   * The key goes in the HEADER only, never a query string (query strings land
#     in access logs, proxy logs and browser history).
#   * Rate limited to 30 requests/minute per IP.
#   * SERVE THIS OVER TLS ONLY, and restrict it at the network/nginx layer to
#     the hosts that legitimately need it. The response body is a live trading
#     credential.
#   * This is deliberately NOT the site passcode: a machine credential that
#     leaks must be revocable without logging every operator out.
#
# Generate one with:  openssl rand -base64 48
# [SECRET · OPTIONAL. Unset simply disables GET /api/tokens/*.]
# → belongs in the PROTECTED SECRETS FILE (/etc/gts/secrets.env, mode 0600), NOT in .env.
TOKEN_EXPOSURE_KEY=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Zerodha token provider (src/tokens/*) — ONLY USED WHEN BROKER_LOGIN_MODE=provider
#
# Fetches the day's access token from the external CalSpread token route using a
# shared passcode. Ignored entirely in the default in_app mode.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The Zerodha token endpoint. NO DEFAULT, deliberately — see below.
#
# ⚠ THERE IS NO DEFAULT AND MUST NOT BE. This route receives a BROKER ACCESS TOKEN that will be
#   used to place real orders, and the passcode below travels to it. A default hostname here means
#   a deployment that switches to `provider` mode contacts a host its operator never chose. This
#   previously defaulted to an external third-party deployment's URL; provider mode now REFUSES TO
#   START unless the active broker's URL is set explicitly.
#
#   Set it only to a token service YOU control. If you do not run one, leave this blank and use
#   BROKER_LOGIN_MODE=in_app (the default), where an operator signs in from the workspace instead.
KITE_TOKEN_BROKER_URL=

# The shared passcode for the external CalSpread token routes. Required to fetch a token.
# [SECRET · required: LIVE when zerodha AND BROKER_LOGIN_MODE=provider.]
# → belongs in the PROTECTED SECRETS FILE (/etc/gts/secrets.env, mode 0600), NOT in .env.
KITE_TOKEN_BROKER_PASSCODE=

# If set, the fetched token's api_key must equal this or it is rejected as a
# foreign token. Optional but strongly recommended in production.
KITE_API_KEY_EXPECTED=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dhan token provider (src/tokens/*) — ONLY USED WHEN BROKER_LOGIN_MODE=provider
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# the external CalSpread Dhan token endpoint.
# NO DEFAULT — same reasoning as KITE_TOKEN_BROKER_URL above. Set only a host you control.
DHAN_TOKEN_URL=

# The shared passcode for the external CalSpread Dhan token route. Required to fetch a token.
# [SECRET · required: LIVE when dhan AND BROKER_LOGIN_MODE=provider.]
# → belongs in the PROTECTED SECRETS FILE (/etc/gts/secrets.env, mode 0600), NOT in .env.
DHAN_TOKEN_BROKER_PASSCODE=

# If set, the fetched token's client_id must equal this or it is rejected.
DHAN_CLIENT_ID_EXPECTED=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Token scheduling (src/tokens/*)
#
# The morning poll that acquires the day's token for the active broker.
# ONLY USED WHEN BROKER_LOGIN_MODE=provider.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# IST wall-clock time (HH:MM) the poll starts. Default 09:00.
BROKER_TOKEN_POLL_START=09:00

# How often (ms) to re-poll until a fresh token is obtained. Default 60000.
BROKER_TOKEN_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=60000

# Per-request timeout (ms) for a token fetch. Default 10000.
BROKER_TOKEN_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=10000


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Broker selection
#
# Exactly ONE broker is active at a time. Zerodha and Dhan are never both live.
#
# Which broker a FRESH deployment starts on. Honoured only when PostgreSQL holds no
# active_broker row: restore() always overrides it from the durable record, because
# the operator's last real selection outranks a config default and a restart must
# never silently revert a broker switch. So this decides the FIRST boot and nothing
# else. Unset means zerodha, which is the specified morning default. An unrecognised
# value warns and falls back to zerodha rather than failing startup.
DEFAULT_ACTIVE_BROKER=zerodha

# May losing Zerodha move SPECULATIVE ENTRY to Dhan on its own?
#
# KEEP THIS FALSE. With it false, an unavailable Zerodha is reported as unavailable
# and Dhan is reported "ready — standby": the system does NOT start scanning or
# entering on Dhan, because changing venue is a trading decision and belongs to an
# operator. Read once at construction, so it cannot change mid-session.
#
# It never affects REDUCTION. Exits, protective cancellation, emergency residual
# flattening and reconciliation always run through whichever broker owns the
# exposure, regardless of this flag.
AUTO_FALLBACK_TO_DHAN=false

# A broker SWITCH is always an explicit, blocker-checked, operator-driven action via
# POST /api/broker/select. Neither variable above can perform one.


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared Box live gates (src/box/config.ts, src/config.ts) — GATE 1
#
# These two must BOTH be explicit before any real order is possible.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# paper_latency | paper_legging | live_parity | live. Default paper_latency.
# BOX_EXECUTION_MODE=live additionally REQUIRES BOX_LIVE_TRADING_ENABLED=true or
# the loader refuses to start.
BOX_EXECUTION_MODE=paper_latency

# The shared live master switch. Default false. Must be true before live mode is
# even loadable, and it is only one of several gates.
BOX_LIVE_TRADING_ENABLED=false


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Broker-specific live gates (src/brokers/registry.ts) — GATE 2 (per broker)
#
# Even with GATE 1 explicit, the ACTIVE broker's own gate must be true.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
ZERODHA_LIVE_TRADING_ENABLED=false
DHAN_LIVE_TRADING_ENABLED=false


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dhan safety (src/brokers/dhan/http.ts, src/brokers/registry.ts,
#              src/brokers/dhan/charges.ts)
#
# Dhan order submission is IP-whitelisted at the broker. The static-IP guard
# refuses to go live from an unexpected egress IP.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The whitelisted egress IP this deployment must present to Dhan.
DHAN_STATIC_PUBLIC_IP=

# Require that the observed egress IP matches DHAN_STATIC_PUBLIC_IP. Default true.
DHAN_STATIC_IP_EXPECTED=true

# Enable the Dhan market-data API. Default TRUE: an unset/empty value is treated
# as enabled (src/brokers/registry.ts). Set to false to disable the Dhan feed.
# (This shipped file pins it to false explicitly, keeping a verbatim copy quiet.)
DHAN_DATA_ENABLED=false

# Dhan client id (non-secret identifier). Falls back to the token session's id.
DHAN_CLIENT_ID=

# Dhan app credentials. Required for Dhan LIVE readiness: readDhanCredentials()
# (src/brokers/dhan/auth.ts) is consulted by the Dhan health/problems check, and
# if either is unset Dhan reports "not configured" and live is blocked.
#
# THEY ARE ALSO THE CREDENTIALS FOR THE in_app BROWSER LOGIN. DHAN_API_SECRET is
# sent as a request header (see DHAN_AUTH_SECRET_HEADER) on the two consent calls
# and never leaves this process. Leave empty for a Zerodha-only or paper
# deployment. Never commit real values.
# [SECRET · required: LIVE when DEFAULT_ACTIVE_BROKER=dhan and BROKER_LOGIN_MODE=in_app.]
# → belongs in the PROTECTED SECRETS FILE (/etc/gts/secrets.env, mode 0600), NOT in .env.
DHAN_API_KEY=
# [SECRET · required: LIVE when DEFAULT_ACTIVE_BROKER=dhan and BROKER_LOGIN_MODE=in_app.]
# → belongs in the PROTECTED SECRETS FILE (/etc/gts/secrets.env, mode 0600), NOT in .env.
DHAN_API_SECRET=

# Per-HTTP-call timeout (ms) to Dhan. Default 8000. Range 500..60000.
DHAN_HTTP_TIMEOUT_MS=8000

# Minimum spacing (ms) between Dhan HTTP calls (broker-side pacing). Default 120.
# Range 0..5000. A smaller Box-level value cannot beat this floor.
DHAN_MIN_INTERVAL_MS=120

# Max read attempts for a Dhan order-status read. Default 3. Range 1..6.
DHAN_MAX_READ_ATTEMPTS=3

# --- Dhan charge rate card (src/brokers/dhan/charges.ts) ---
# These reproduce Dhan's contract note. Only brokerage is a genuine broker
# choice; the rest are statutory and match the Zerodha card because they ARE the
# same statutory rates.
DHAN_BROKERAGE_PER_ORDER=20
DHAN_BROKERAGE_MAX_PCT=0
DHAN_STT_SELL_PCT=0.1
DHAN_STT_ROUND_NEAREST_RUPEE=true
DHAN_EXCHANGE_TXN_PCT=0.03503
DHAN_IPFT_PER_CRORE=50
DHAN_SEBI_PCT=0.0001
DHAN_STAMP_DUTY_BUY_PCT=0.003
DHAN_GST_PCT=18
DHAN_CHARGE_RATE_VERSION=dhan-nse-options-2026-04-01


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Zerodha charge rate card (src/box/localCharges.ts)
#
# The local charge estimator used when Zerodha is the active broker. Every value
# below is read by loadLocalChargeRates(); defaults are the code fallbacks. Only
# brokerage is a genuine broker choice — the statutory heads match the Dhan card
# because they ARE the same statutory rates (note STT here defaults to 0.15,
# vs the Dhan card's 0.1).
BOX_STT_TYPE=stt
BOX_CHARGE_RATE_VERSION=zerodha-nse-options-2026-04-01
BOX_BROKERAGE_PER_ORDER=20
BOX_BROKERAGE_MAX_PCT=0
BOX_STT_SELL_PCT=0.15
BOX_STT_ROUND_NEAREST_RUPEE=true
BOX_EXCHANGE_TXN_PCT=0.03503
BOX_IPFT_PER_CRORE=50
BOX_IPFT_PCT=0
BOX_SEBI_PCT=0.0001
BOX_STAMP_DUTY_BUY_PCT=0.003
BOX_GST_PCT=18


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mongo projection — the async reporting REPLICA (src/outbox/mongo.ts)
#
# MongoDB Atlas is a reporting replica, never on the execution hot path and never
# required before a broker POST. A missing URI or an Atlas outage is a non-event
# for everything except the background projector. It is NOT the backup of record;
# PostgreSQL is (see docs/DEPLOYMENT.md).
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Atlas connection string (include the db name in the path). If blank, export is
# disabled and the projector never starts. Never logged.
# [SECRET · required: only when MONGO_EXPORT_ENABLED=true. Carries the database password.]
# → belongs in the PROTECTED SECRETS FILE (/etc/gts/secrets.env, mode 0600), NOT in .env.
MONGODB_URI=

# Master switch for export. Default true — but export only actually runs when
# this is true AND MONGODB_URI is set.
MONGO_EXPORT_ENABLED=true

# Rows drained per batch. Default 100.
MONGO_EXPORT_BATCH_SIZE=100

# Drain interval (ms). Default 1000.
MONGO_EXPORT_INTERVAL_MS=1000

# Max backoff (ms) when Atlas is failing. Default 60000.
MONGO_EXPORT_MAX_BACKOFF_MS=60000

# Bounded server-selection timeout (ms) so a dead Atlas fails the drain fast.
# Default 5000.
MONGO_EXPORT_SERVER_SELECTION_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# Attempt budget before a poison row is dead-lettered. Default 10.
MONGO_EXPORT_MAX_ATTEMPTS=10

# Explicit target db name; defaults to the db parsed from MONGODB_URI, or
# "strikedge" when the URI carries no path.
#
# The fallback name is unchanged by the rebrand for the same reason as DATABASE_URL:
# it names a real database that already holds the reporting replica. Set this
# explicitly if you want a different one.
MONGO_EXPORT_DB=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Legacy import (src/scripts/migrateBoxFromMongo.ts)
#
# ONE-TIME cutover tool: reads the old the predecessor codebase Box collections and writes them
# into this backend's PostgreSQL. Dry-run by default. See docs/LEGACY_IMPORT.md.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Source (legacy) Box MongoDB connection string. Read-only for the import.
# [SECRET · required: only by the one-off Mongo import script.]
# → belongs in the PROTECTED SECRETS FILE (/etc/gts/secrets.env, mode 0600), NOT in .env.
LEGACY_BOX_MONGODB_URI=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reservation identity (src/box/reservations/identity.ts)
#
# Namespaces durable reservations so staging and production — or two workers —
# never take each other's locks. DEVELOPMENT AND PRODUCTION MUST NOT SHARE ONE.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# THE `CALSPREAD_` PREFIX ON THESE TWO NAMES IS COMPATIBILITY-SENSITIVE AND WAS NOT
# RENAMED BY THE REBRAND. CALSPREAD_DEPLOYMENT_ID is the namespace component of the
# DURABLE OWNER ID stored in the PostgreSQL reservation rows, and owner-verified
# release compares that id byte-for-byte. Rename the variable and a restarted
# process resolves a different namespace: it stops recognising its own outstanding
# reservations and could release or renew a lease it does not own. That is a
# multi-process safety mechanism, not a label. Set the values; keep the names.

# Lock namespace. Explicit value wins; otherwise falls back to NODE_ENV.
CALSPREAD_DEPLOYMENT_ID=

# Host/replica label used in owner ids and conflict logs. Defaults to hostname.
CALSPREAD_INSTANCE_ID=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PostgreSQL naming (src/pg/pool.ts)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# application_name shown in pg_stat_activity. Default gts. Cosmetic — it only labels
# this deployment's connections. If any monitoring query FILTERS on it, update that
# query or pin the old value here.
PG_APPLICATION_NAME=gts


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Broker token key rotation (src/scripts/rotateBrokerTokenKey.ts)
#
# Used ONLY by `npm run rotate:broker-token-key`. Not read by the running server.
# Both must decode to 32 bytes (same encodings as BROKER_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY).
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [SECRET · required: only by `npm run rotate:broker-token-key`. Never by the server.]
# → belongs in the PROTECTED SECRETS FILE (/etc/gts/secrets.env, mode 0600), NOT in .env.
BROKER_TOKEN_OLD_KEY=
# [SECRET · required: only by `npm run rotate:broker-token-key`. Never by the server.]
# → belongs in the PROTECTED SECRETS FILE (/etc/gts/secrets.env, mode 0600), NOT in .env.
BROKER_TOKEN_NEW_KEY=


# =============================================================================
# Box strategy configuration (src/box/config.ts)
#
# Every value below is OPTIONAL — the defaults ARE the shipped specification and
# keep execution in paper_latency. Grouped by concern; defaults shown are the code
# fallbacks.
#
# LEAVE A KNOB EMPTY TO USE ITS DEFAULT. DO NOT WRITE 0 TO MEAN "DEFAULT".
# An explicit 0 is a real value: the loader reads it and honours it. For the timing
# knobs that is actively harmful, because several are passed straight to setInterval
# and 0 there means "run as fast as the event loop allows", not "disabled" — which
# starves the market-data drain (no depth, feed reported down while the socket is
# open) and shows up as intermittent 502s from the reverse proxy.
#
# This file previously shipped `=0` for fourteen *_MS knobs that have non-zero code
# defaults, so copying it verbatim silently replaced every one of those defaults with
# zero. They are now blank. `periodMs()` in src/box/config.ts additionally refuses a
# non-positive timer period and warns, so the trap cannot be re-armed by hand — but
# the knobs whose 0 legitimately means "unbounded" or "disabled" (BOX_MAX_UNDERLYINGS,
# BOX_MAX_CROSS_LEG_EXCHANGE_DISPERSION_MS) still read 0
# as that, and are deliberately left as they were.
# =============================================================================

# --- Entry gate & edges ---
# Minimum GROSS edge (₹) from the spread alone — a cheap prefilter. Charges are
# recorded/displayed but NOT deducted before deciding to enter.
MIN_BOX_GROSS_EDGE=1200
# Optional NET floor (₹). 0 disables it (gross gate is the only gate).
MIN_BOX_NET_EDGE=0
# Skip a box whose charges could not be priced (net exit rules need them). Default true.
BOX_REQUIRE_PRICED_CHARGES=true
# Conservative slippage/safety allowance (₹) per round trip, in the NET figure only.
BOX_SAFETY_BUFFER=150
# A deliberate LOWER bound (₹) on round-trip charges, used only by the net prefilter.
BOX_PREFILTER_CHARGE_ALLOWANCE=160
# THE ENTRY GATE proper: minimum projected NET profit (₹) after every cost. Default 1200.
BOX_MIN_EXPECTED_NET_PROFIT=1200
# Auto-exit floor: a box closes only once current net P&L (₹) reaches at least this. Default 600.
BOX_MIN_EXIT_NET_PNL=600

# --- Book freshness ---
# How long (ms) an UNCHANGED order book is still trusted (NOT a price-age limit). Default 15000.
BOX_QUOTE_MAX_AGE_MS=15000
# Max age (ms) of the newest tick across the WHOLE universe — catches a dead feed. Default 5000.
BOX_FEED_MAX_AGE_MS=5000
# Max age (ms) of the underlying used to place the ATM window. Default 10000.
BOX_UNDERLYING_MAX_AGE_MS=10000

# --- ATM window / universe ---
BOX_ATM_HYSTERESIS=0.15
BOX_WINDOW_MIN_INTERVAL_MS=15000
BOX_STRIKE_LEVEL=2
BOX_MAX_UNDERLYINGS=0
BOX_MAX_SUBSCRIBED_TOKENS=0
BOX_MAX_PUBLISHED_OPPORTUNITIES=0
BOX_UNIVERSE_REFRESH_MS=
BOX_ENABLE_SHORT_BOX=false
BOX_DEFAULT_TICK_SIZE=0.05
# BOX_EXPIRY_SAFETY_MINUTES=          # 0 DISABLES the expiry safety window. Unset = 45 minutes.
BOX_CONVERGENCE_PCT=0
BOX_CONVERGENCE_FLOOR=0
BOX_PROFIT_CAPTURE_PCT=0
BOX_MIN_CAPTURED_PCT=0
BOX_EXIT_USE_REALISABLE=false
# BOX_EXPECTED_ENTRY_SLIPPAGE=        # 0 removes modelled cost, which LOOSENS the entry gate. Unset = 250.
# BOX_EXPECTED_EXIT_SLIPPAGE=         # 0 removes modelled cost, which LOOSENS the entry gate. Unset = 250.
BOX_INDICATIVE_DISCOVERY=false
BOX_INDICATIVE_MAX_UNDERLYINGS=0
BOX_INDICATIVE_REFRESH_MS=

# --- Scanning / publishing intervals ---
BOX_PUBLISH_INTERVAL_MS=
BOX_PERSIST_INTERVAL_MS=
BOX_MONITOR_INTERVAL_MS=
BOX_METRICS_WINDOW=0
BOX_PAPER_PERSISTENCE_MS=0

# --- Concurrency & per-underlying limits ---
BOX_MAX_CONCURRENT_EXECUTIONS=0
# BOX_MAX_CONCURRENT_PER_UNDERLYING=  # 0 DISABLES the per-underlying execution budget. Unset = 2.
BOX_ONE_ACTIVE_BOX_PER_UNDERLYING=false
BOX_INTERNAL_NETTING_ENABLED=false
BOX_SESSION_MAX_COMPLETED_TRADES=0
# Maximum ENTRY ATTEMPTS one armed session may START. 0 = unbounded (default).
#
# BOUNDS RISK-TAKING, NOT SUCCESS. BOX_SESSION_MAX_COMPLETED_TRADES counts cycles consumed when a
# full four-leg Box is ESTABLISHED, so an attempt that submitted orders, partially filled and was then
# unwound or recovered spends no cycle -- correctly, because burning a permitted trade on an attempt
# that left no position would be indefensible. But on its own that bounded nothing: a trial set to one
# trade could submit orders indefinitely as long as no attempt ever completed, taking real exposure and
# paying real charges each time. This ceiling is counted at ADMISSION, before any broker POST, so a
# failed or recovered attempt still spends it. For a bounded one-lot trial, set BOTH.
BOX_SESSION_MAX_ENTRY_ATTEMPTS=0

# --- THE SUPERVISED ONE-LOT TRIAL PROFILE ---
#
# Opt-in. Unset/false = this is a general-purpose deployment and NOTHING below is required.
#
# true REQUIRES all FOUR of the following to be exactly 1, and REFUSES TO START otherwise:
#
#   BOX_MAX_OPEN_BOXES=1                 coordinator prologue, every mode. Counts durable inventory
#                                        PLUS in-flight entry claims, so it is the only gate that can
#                                        refuse two entries admitted in the same instant.
#   BOX_LIVE_MAX_OPEN_BOXES=1            BoxOrderManager, live only, at the send boundary.
#   BOX_SESSION_MAX_COMPLETED_TRADES=1   counts CONSUMED cycles, so it keeps refusing AFTER the first
#                                        box CLOSES. No inventory ceiling can: a closed box leaves
#                                        no inventory.
#   BOX_SESSION_MAX_ENTRY_ATTEMPTS=1     the ONLY bound on an ABORTED attempt (rejected, unwound or
#                                        recovered). Such an attempt completes no cycle and leaves no
#                                        inventory, so nothing else counts it.
#
# WHY ALL FOUR, AND WHY A PROFILE RATHER THAN NEW DEFAULTS. Three of the four default to 0 = UNLIMITED,
# so "configure a one-shot trial" is three chances to leave a bound off with no feedback at all. But
# changing those defaults to 1 would silently convert every existing paper deployment into a one-shot
# one, which is worse than the problem. So the bounds are mandatory only inside this profile.
#
# The flag itself changes NO behaviour. It makes the four settings mandatory and visible.
#
# VERIFY BEFORE ARMING, on the host, with the .env loaded:
#
#   node dist/box/effectiveConfig.js --supervised-trial --lot-size=<N>
#
# <N> is the CURRENT lot size from the LIVE instrument master for the contract being traded. It is not
# optional and it is not guessable: the per-leg and gross quantities scale entirely with it, the test
# fixtures use 75 and an earlier note guessed 65. Omit it and the report says UNVERIFIED rather than
# printing plausible arithmetic. See docs/SUPERVISED_ONE_LOT_GATE.md.
BOX_SUPERVISED_ONE_LOT_TRIAL=false

# --- Charge estimation & reconciliation ---
BOX_CHARGE_CACHE_TTL_MS=
BOX_CHARGE_CONCURRENCY=0
BOX_RECONCILE_CHARGES=false
BOX_CHARGE_RECONCILE_CONCURRENCY=0
BOX_CHARGE_RECONCILE_MAX_ATTEMPTS=0
BOX_CHARGE_RECONCILE_RETRY_BASE_MS=
BOX_CHARGE_RECONCILE_WARN_PCT=0

# --- Closed-trade cache (PostgreSQL-backed; no Redis) ---
BOX_CLOSED_CACHE_ENABLED=false
BOX_CLOSED_CACHE_TTL_SEC=0

# --- P&L snapshot / archive (PostgreSQL-backed; no Redis) ---
BOX_PNL_ARCHIVE_HOUR=0
BOX_PNL_ARCHIVE_DRAIN_DELAY_MS=

# --- Market feed ---
# The dedicated Box market-data lane: ONE socket on the ACTIVE broker. Default true.
BOX_DEDICATED_MARKET_FEED=true

# --- Durable reservations (multi-worker safety) ---
BOX_DURABLE_RESERVATIONS_ENABLED=true
BOX_RESERVATION_REQUIRE_DURABLE=false
BOX_RESERVATION_RENEW_INTERVAL_MS=0
# BOX_RESERVATION_OWNERSHIP_MARGIN_MS= # 0 collapses the ownership dead band. Unset = 1500.
# BOX_RESERVATION_CLOCK_SKEW_GRACE_MS= # 0 collapses the skew grace. Unset = 250.
BOX_RESERVATION_UNCERTAIN_HOLD_MAX_MS=0
BOX_INSTRUMENT_LOCK_TTL_MS=0

# --- Execution coordinator / scheduling ---
# BOX_EXECUTION_COORDINATOR_ENABLED=  # false DISABLES shared-contract exclusion. Unset = true.
BOX_EXECUTION_MAX_WAIT_MS=
BOX_EXECUTION_POLL_MS=
BOX_EXECUTION_TIMING_METRICS_ENABLED=false
BOX_EXECUTION_TIMING_WINDOW=0
# Max cross-leg EXCHANGE-TIMESTAMP dispersion (ms). Unset = 1000. 0 DISABLES this constraint, in
# live too (receive-time below still governs).
#
# MUST NOT BE SET BELOW THE BROKER'S STAMP PRECISION. Kite stamps books in epoch SECONDS, so
# cross-leg dispersion can only ever be 0, 1000, 2000 … ms — four books published 160 ms apart
# measure 0 ms inside one second and a full 1000 ms across a boundary. A limit under 1000 therefore
# refuses coherent books on quantisation alone: it is unsatisfiable, not strict. (The default was
# 250 and did exactly that, refusing 100% of entries on a healthy feed.) The engine now WARNS at
# boot if this is below the active broker's precision. Treat this as a coarse sanity check — it
# still catches genuinely separated stamps such as 15s/18s/15s/19s = 4000 ms — and use the
# receive-time gate below for sub-second coherence, since that one is measured on our own
# millisecond arrival clock.
BOX_MAX_CROSS_LEG_EXCHANGE_DISPERSION_MS=1000
# Max cross-leg RECEIVE-TIME dispersion (ms) — the ALWAYS-available live coherence
# gate (Dhan supplies no book exchange timestamp; Kite's is 1s-granular). Default 500.
# IMPORTANT: in LIVE, 0 is IMPOSSIBLE-TO-SATISFY (it rejects every multi-leg entry),
# NOT a silent bypass — so an unconfigured operator can never accidentally disable
# cross-leg coherence. To truly disable it in live, set
# BOX_COHERENCE_ZERO_DISPERSION_DISABLES_IN_LIVE=true below. PAPER always reads 0 as
# "disabled". (BOX_MAX_CROSS_LEG_EXCHANGE_DISPERSION_MS=0 above disables ONLY the
# exchange-timestamp constraint; receive-time still governs.)
BOX_MAX_CROSS_LEG_RECEIVE_DISPERSION_MS=500
# Max plausible (received_at - exchange_at) in ms; larger is a feed/clock fault. Default 5000.
BOX_MAX_RECEIVE_TO_EXCHANGE_DELAY_MS=5000
# Explicit opt-out: read a 0 cross-leg dispersion limit as "disabled" in LIVE. Default false.
BOX_COHERENCE_ZERO_DISPERSION_DISABLES_IN_LIVE=false
# BOX_CONFLICT_REVALIDATE_MIN_EDGE_RATIO= # 0 DISABLES post-conflict edge re-validation. Unset = 0.8.
BOX_CONFLICT_WAIT_MAX_MS=0

# --- Leg execution ---
# parallel | sequential. Default parallel.
BOX_LEG_EXECUTION_MODE=parallel
BOX_LEG_MAX_CHASE_TICKS=0
BOX_LEG_TIMEOUT_MS=
BOX_LEG_UNWIND_LATENCY_MS=
BOX_UNWIND_MAX_CHASE_TICKS=0
BOX_QUEUE_MODEL=
BOX_QUEUE_LIQUIDITY_HAIRCUT_PCT=0

# --- Tuning surface ---
# (BOX_TUNING_KEYS / BOX_TUNING_LIMITS are NOT environment variables: they are
# internal constants in src/box/config.ts describing the runtime-tuning API
# surface — the tunables minExpectedNetProfit / safetyBuffer are set at runtime,
# not via .env. See docs/DOC_AUDIT.md A.1.)

# --- Regions (calibration labels; null unless set) ---
BOX_DEPLOYMENT_REGION=
BOX_EXECUTION_CALIBRATION_REGION=

# =============================================================================
# The ten calibration / live-timing variables (documented in all four required
# places: this file, README.md, docs/CONFIGURATION.md, src/box/LIVE_EXECUTION.md).
# Defaults read from src/box/config.ts.
# =============================================================================

# Paper calibration: minimum samples before the calibrated latency source is
# trusted overall. Default 30 (clamped 5..100000).
BOX_PAPER_CALIBRATION_MIN_SAMPLES=30
# Minimum samples PER time bucket. Default 60 (clamped 5..100000).
BOX_PAPER_CALIBRATION_BUCKET_MIN_SAMPLES=60
# Max age (ms) a calibration sample stays eligible. Default 604800000 (7 days).
BOX_PAPER_CALIBRATION_MAX_AGE_MS=604800000
# Bucket calibration by time-of-day. Default true.
BOX_PAPER_CALIBRATION_TIME_BUCKETS=true
# Simulated cancel latency (ms) in paper. Default 150 (clamped 0..60000).
BOX_PAPER_CANCEL_LATENCY_MS=150
# Persist live execution timing samples to PostgreSQL. Default false.
BOX_LIVE_TIMING_PERSIST_ENABLED=false
# Rows per live-timing flush batch. Default 50 (clamped 1..10000).
BOX_LIVE_TIMING_BATCH_SIZE=50
# Live-timing flush interval (ms). Default 15000 (clamped 250..600000).
BOX_LIVE_TIMING_FLUSH_MS=15000
# Emit event-loop lag metrics for the execution path. Default true.
BOX_EXECUTION_EVENT_LOOP_METRICS_ENABLED=true
# Shadow mode: run live logic against paper fills with no broker contact. Default
# false. Cannot be combined with BOX_EXECUTION_MODE=live.
BOX_SHADOW_MODE_ENABLED=false

# --- Paper latency model ---
BOX_PAPER_EXECUTION_PROFILE=standard
BOX_PAPER_LATENCY_MODE=constant
BOX_PAPER_LATENCY_SAMPLES=
BOX_PAPER_LATENCY_SEED=
BOX_PAPER_LATENCY_ACK_TERMINAL_SAMPLES=
BOX_SIMULATED_LATENCY_MS=
BOX_SIMULATED_DECISION_MS=
BOX_PAPER_MAX_BOX_CAPITAL_RUPEES=0
BOX_PAPER_MAX_CONCURRENT_EXECUTIONS=0

# --- Live execution limits (only consulted when live is fully gated + armed) ---
#
# ⚠ BOX_LIVE_MAX_BOX_CAPITAL_RUPEES=0 MEANS NO ₹ CEILING, AND LIVE NOW REFUSES TO BOOT WITH IT.
#
#   0 is fine here because this file is the PAPER-safe default and never enables live. But it is the
#   only MONETARY containment on a single Box, and the two quantity ceilings are not a substitute:
#   they bound LOTS, and a Box can satisfy both while still committing an arbitrary rupee amount,
#   because notional is price × quantity.
#
#   The two quantity ceilings already refused 0 at boot (their minimum is 1). This one could be left
#   at its shipped value and silently removed — so with BOX_EXECUTION_MODE=live it is now refused
#   too, and all three agree. Set it to the largest amount ONE Box may commit before promoting to
#   live; 100000 is a reasonable starting figure for a one-lot NIFTY test, and every deploy/ live
#   template already sets it.
BOX_LIVE_MAX_OPEN_BOXES=0
BOX_LIVE_MAX_CONCURRENT_EXECUTIONS=0
BOX_LIVE_MAX_BOX_CAPITAL_RUPEES=0
# BOX_LIVE_MAX_OPEN_LEG_QUANTITY=     # Set to EXACTLY one lot of your instrument. 0 is refused at boot.
# BOX_LIVE_MAX_GROSS_OPEN_LEG_QUANTITY= # Set to EXACTLY four lots. 0 is refused at boot.
BOX_LIVE_MAX_RESIDUAL_LEGS=0
BOX_LIVE_MAX_CHASE_TICKS=0
BOX_LIVE_MAX_MODIFICATIONS=0
# BOX_LIVE_DAILY_LOSS_LIMIT=          # 0 DISABLES the daily-loss circuit breaker. Unset = 5000.
BOX_LIVE_CONSECUTIVE_FAILURE_LIMIT=0
BOX_LIVE_REJECT_LIMIT=0
BOX_LIVE_ACK_TIMEOUT_MS=0
BOX_LIVE_CANCEL_TIMEOUT_MS=0
BOX_LIVE_WORKING_TIMEOUT_MS=0
BOX_LIVE_PARTIAL_TIMEOUT_MS=0
# Absolute end-to-end budget for ONE live order mutation (ms), started BEFORE queue admission:
# adapter pacer + broker HTTP pacing queue + network + response body all draw on this one budget.
# A mutation whose budget lapses while queued is released promptly and provably transmits nothing.
BOX_LIVE_ORDER_MUTATION_DEADLINE_MS=0
BOX_LIVE_HTTP_TIMEOUT_MS=0
BOX_LIVE_RECONCILE_INTERVAL_MS=0
BOX_LIVE_ENTRY_SUBMIT_CONCURRENCY=0
BOX_LIVE_BROKER_MIN_INTERVAL_MS=0
BOX_LIVE_BROKER_ORDER_MIN_INTERVAL_MS=0
# BOX_LIVE_FEED_RECONNECT_WARMUP_MS=  # 0 DISABLES the post-reconnect quiet period. Unset = 5000.
